```

The code above shows a framework for contract tests on a ArrayList. There are three contracts (methods):
* AddContract
* RemoveContract
* InsertContract

Each of these contracts covers a different member method of a class, checks the precondition of the method (input), executes the method, and observes the postcondition of the method (output)

These methods are annotated with the [PexMethod] attribute and [PexAssumeUnderTest] is used to indicate which method under test is being targeted.

This framework will be able to generate a great deal of test data by randomly generating input data, thus improving the efficiency of finding bugs. Also, the execution of these contracts gives more visibility on the flow of the program and helps to identify incorrect assumptions or omissions in the program.

For more information, refer to Pex’s online version.

Remember that Pex can only find bugs for the methods that it is run on. It will not find bugs for methods that are part of the program’s normal flow. Use it in the areas that you suspect might have bugs.
Further Reading and References:

Pex Documentation: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/pex/
NUnit API Documentation: https://nunit.org/nuget/
Microsoft FxCop: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/code-quality/install-fxcop-analyzers?view=vs-2019
Visual Studio Code: https://code.visualstudio.com/

# Bugs
Bugs should be minimal, and it should be a simple way to reproduce the issue. Ensure all team members have access to the code and can replicate the issue.
Bugs should ideally be tested on a non-production environment. If it is tested on a production environment, it should first be fixed and then deployed. 

# Project Structure

You should have a clean and consistent project structure, and your project should follow the structure discussed in the lecture. Some commonly used project structures are discussed below.

## Monolithic Project Structure

* All components, utilities, and plugins are stored in libraries within the project.
* The project is structured in a way that allows for clear understanding of the functionality and how different parts of the project interact with each other.
* For small projects, a monolithic structure is simple, straightforward, and doesn't add much complexity.
* However, for larger projects, if the codebase grows significantly, it can become difficult to handle.

## Microservices-based Structure

* Each part of the Software System has its own functionality and can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently.
* It allows for high agility and scalability.
* Each microservice can be written in the technology specific to the task, which encourages the development of a polyglot architecture.
* However, managing and coordinating interactions between such microservices can become complex.

Remember, there's no definitive right answer. The structure of the project should be chosen based on your project needs, team preferences, and project size.

# Source Control

Most team start using distributed version control systems like Git or Mercurial that offer many advantages over centralized systems like Subversion (SVN).

Make sure you have the following in place:
* A main branch (typically 'master') that represents the production-ready state of your project.
* A feature branching model where developers create their own branches to work on new features.
* Regular commits and pushes to ensure that your team's work is reflected in the central repository.
* Proper branch management including merging and rebasing practices.
* Use proper commit messages to provide context about the changes done in the commits.

# Continuous Integration (CI)

Use CI to automate the build and testing of your application as soon as changes are committed to your repository. Automated build and tests make it easier to discover and rectify issues before the code gets released into production.

Popular CI tools include Jenkins, Travis CI, and GitHub Actions.

# Continuous Delivery (CD) CI
Continuous Delivery is a set of practices and techniques to automatically build, test and deploy software. It encompasses the practices of Continuous Integration and also extend to deploying to production.

